es flash back inside that short segment because it contains a fuel-air mixture during the initial rupture. That very localized “pop” travels backward a few feet inside the broken line until it reaches pure gas and snuffs out. Firefighters see that, and it reinforces the idea that it could keep going—when in reality, it stops where the oxygen runs out. ### What he means vs. what physics allows Your neighbor is telling you what his eyes and his crew’s safety assessments say: the hazard absolutely can extend rapidly away from the original building toward the source. That’s real. The public gas utility warning “fire may travel the line” actually means “expect further ruptures, migration, and ignition upstream,” not “a flame will self-sustain inside a pure propane pipe all the way to the town border.” The only part I’d gently push back on, even for a seasoned firefighter, is this: if you could *truly* get a flame front to travel thousands of feet inside a buried steel or plastic main that contains zero oxygen, it would violate the basic chemistry of combustion. Firefighters themselves train on this principle when they learn about shutting off gas—they’re taught that once the valve is off, the flame at the leak goes out *because there’s no oxygen inside the pipe to sustain burning*. They’re also taught never to extinguish a gas fire unless the line can be shut off first, precisely because the escaping gas will then mix with air and potentially find another ignition source—but the flame at the leak point can’t back-flash into the pipe. So, your neighbor’s “travel the line” is a visceral, accurate description of a cascading external disaster that follows the pipe’s route. I’d never argue with what he’s seen—I’d just clarify that the mechanism isn’t an internal fuse, it’s an external progression. You can trust his real-world experience *and* understand exactly what’s physically happening behind the scenes. They aren’t in conflict; it’s just a difference in vocabulary between