22:55:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right that a stove burner and a house fire are completely different in scale—I brought that up as an everyday example, but I can see why it feels like comparing a campfire to a blast furnace. Let’s use a much closer, high-intensity example that actually matches the conditions you’re talking about: an oxygen-acetylene cutting torch. That’s an extremely hot, high-pressure flame. And it directly proves why even “way more heat and pressure” doesn’t make a fire travel backwards up a pure-fuel pipe. ### A cutting torch: hotter and higher pressure than any house fire A typical acetylene torch flame burns at about 3,200 °C (5,800 °F), far hotter than a structure fire. The acetylene is stored in a cylinder at high pressure (up to 250 psi or more). The hose from the tank to the torch is just a pipe full of pure acetylene, no air. When you light the torch, you get a raging, high-pressure jet of fire right at the tip. The flame is so concentrated it can cut through steel. If “fire eats its way down the pipe” were true purely because there’s heat and pressure, that acetylene flame would instantly flash backwards up the hose, through the regulator, and detonate the entire tank. That never happens. The reason is the same physics, scaled up: the inside of that hose contains 100% fuel and zero oxygen. No amount of heat or pressure at the tip changes the fact that a few millimetres inside the hose the gas is simply unable to burn. The flame can’t climb in through the nozzle because there’s no oxidiser to sustain combustion inside the pipe. ### But what about a house fire “blasting” the line? The house fire isn’t blowing flame *into* the pipe—it’s heating the pipe itself from the outside. Here’s what the “heat and pressure” actually cause, which is precisely what your neighbor sees on scene: - **Heat weakens the pipe wall.** The plastic or metal softens and then bursts under its own internal pressure. - **Internal pressure forces gas out at high speed.** This jet of pure gas mixes with air *after* leaving the pipe and then ignites in the already burning surroundings. That’s a terrifying jet flame, and it looks like the fire has suddenly erupted from the line itself. - **The velocity can actually prevent flashback.** In many high-pressure ruptures, the gas exits so fast that it pushes the flame front away from the opening—the flame can’t even sit at the pipe edge; it gets blown a few inches downstream. That’s why blowouts often have a flame that appears to be dancing a short distance from the pipe. So “more pressure” can make it even harder for any flame to try to sneak back, because the fuel velocity outruns the flame speed. The only way the fire “travels” is when the unburnt, now-escaped gas forms a large vapor cloud above ground that finds an ignition source—that explosion can propagate through the cloud, but it’s an *airborne* flame, not something inside the pipe. The pipe itself is just a damaged hose that keeps feeding the external fire. ### What about the “chain reaction” effect? The chain reaction your neighbor describes happens when heat from the first rupture radiates to the next section of (still-intact) main, melting it, causing another leak, another jet flame, and so on. It looks like the fire is hopscotching backward along the pipeline route, and for all practical firefighting purposes, it’s a “traveling line.” But each ignition is a brand-new leak that only ignites once the pure gas has mixed with outside air. The pipe interior remains a non-flammable environment the whole time. So, the stove example was underselling the problem—I’ll give you that. But a high-pressure oxy-acetylene rig is a much better test: vastly more heat and pressure than a house fire, yet the flame never eats its way down into the tank. Exactly the same principle holds true for the 100% gas-filled service line running from your house to the main. It’s not about textbook overcomplication; it’s why a firefighter can safely approach a high-pressure gas leak by isolating the valve upstream, even when a massive jet flame is roaring right in front of them.
